{"title":"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 17","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_Different_Approach\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 Different Approach</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_(quick)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 (quick)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(coord_bash)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (coord bash)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>A line that passes through the origin intersects both the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/3/34310f2f36ed6d724838edb08788ee62acb33386.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" /> and the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/5/5c5d0774fabfa66742a85a953f5c0c8a90afc01f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=1+ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"103\" height=\"41\" />. The three lines create an equilateral triangle. What is the perimeter of the triangle?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/b/12be0a6f2cf2144f67b0b2d9dbbafbbf213b7a2d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A)}\\ 2\\sqrt{6} \\qquad\\textbf{(B)} \\ 2+2\\sqrt{3} \\qquad\\textbf{(C)} \\ 6 \\qquad\\textbf{(D)} \\ 3 + 2\\sqrt{3} \\qquad\\textbf{(E)} \\ 6 + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"533\" height=\"41\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since the triangle is equilateral and one of the sides is a vertical line, the triangle must have a horizontal line of symmetry, and therefore the other two sides will have opposite slopes. The slope of the other given line is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/c/e5cb6e7c42d6bf0c332b448f0d28fff6c0aaaef1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"26\" height=\"41\" /> (which is must be, given 60 degree angle of the triangle, relative to vertical) so the third must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4f620cb0047a60cbfff5c979bdb5a528939e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"41\" height=\"41\" />. Since this third line passes through the origin, its equation is simply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/2/b52e445b5152a10f3fbc40809ba0db887e5a4923.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"86\" height=\"41\" />. To find two vertices of the triangle, plug in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/5/4c52e02a35db152495396584bfeae1d0599cd753.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> to both the other equations. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/7/db73a7e4ddec3c87a183b809b9284622f01c639f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"74\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/9/699d303935f2bd03b54f2d56129ea225b19cf028.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = 1 + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"92\" height=\"41\" />\n</p><p>We now have the coordinates of two vertices, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/7/aa78892576d1846de9b191eeca4f169b31051dbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(1, -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"90\" height=\"55\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/2/ce2889bd7b10e0adba5330b14079874f4520b3c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(1, 1 + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"101\" height=\"53\" />. The length of one side is the distance between the y-coordinates, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/3/e8355d01fa2acf82aa8078a6f17800f0cd84cf92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 +  \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"67\" height=\"41\" />.\n</p><p>The perimeter of the triangle is thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/5/6456240e20556fde9f490fb2ad6515c1ffbd8c5b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\left(1 +  \\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -22px\" width=\"105\" height=\"53\" />, so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/4/5449d48993b0316a29237b1e741e74b65c1d29c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }3 + 2\\sqrt{3}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"112\" height=\"33\" />\n</p><p>Note: We know that the slope for the third line must be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4f620cb0047a60cbfff5c979bdb5a528939e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"41\" height=\"41\" />, as the slope for the second given line is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/c/e5cb6e7c42d6bf0c332b448f0d28fff6c0aaaef1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"26\" height=\"41\" />. The slope is determined from two points on a line, and we pick one of the points to be the point where they intersect. Now, drawing the symmetric line, it splits the first line; x=1 into two equal parts. Now, the second point for the third and second lines have the same x coordinate, but opposite y coordinates.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_Different_Approach\">Solution 1 Different Approach</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 Different Approach\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>It is important to note that because \\( \\tan(x) \\) represents the slope of a function, then \\( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\) is just \\( \\tan(30^\\circ) \\). Then, if we reflect \\( \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} x+1 \\) over the line \\( y=\\frac{1}{2} \\), we get \\( y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}x \\) as our new function.\n</p><p>Given that, we may resume with solution 1.\n</p><p>~Pinotation (I did not write solution 1)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Draw a line from the y-intercept of the equation  <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/5/5c5d0774fabfa66742a85a953f5c0c8a90afc01f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=1+ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"103\" height=\"41\" /> perpendicular to the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/5/4c52e02a35db152495396584bfeae1d0599cd753.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />. There is a square of side length 1 inscribed in the equilateral triangle. The problem becomes reduced to finding the perimeter of an equilateral triangle with a square of side length 1 inscribed in it. The side length is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/b/08b496cce5d3378b354c9de77af9321fd94ef76e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\right) + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"96\" height=\"43\" />. After multiplying the side length by 3 and rationalizing, you get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/4/5449d48993b0316a29237b1e741e74b65c1d29c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }3 + 2\\sqrt{3}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"112\" height=\"33\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the intersection point between the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/b/aab2b2353903838034fdd286e775641aed799eb3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = 1 + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"106\" height=\"43\" /> and the line that crosses the origin be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>We drop an altitude from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/4/4b4cade9ca8a2c8311fafcf040bc5b15ca507f52.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> onto the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/3/34310f2f36ed6d724838edb08788ee62acb33386.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" />. Since the overall triangle is an equilateral triangle, we are splitting the base (on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/5/4c52e02a35db152495396584bfeae1d0599cd753.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />) in half. As the y-axis is parallel to the line <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/5/4c52e02a35db152495396584bfeae1d0599cd753.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, the altitude from P will also split the y-axis from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/c/8/ec89f09c84538ec43930ca5d8e5e0957e2000822.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"45\" height=\"17\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/3/6c3b4834614e5e7e189cb7d621cfb4fb0bd36dd7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> in half. From this, we can get that the y-value of P is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/9/2f960094315d60883495f9b74148e17487ee9584.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>Plugging this into the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/b/aab2b2353903838034fdd286e775641aed799eb3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y = 1 + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"106\" height=\"43\" />, we get that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/7/2678067a3ad6205b848c41054ef38efd652eaf3d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"79\" height=\"43\" />, and thus our height for the equilateral triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/a/b/7ab7d7b3a3ca58b430b2d4f05364b3f3a2200920.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 + \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"61\" height=\"43\" />. Using that, we can calculate the perimeter to be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/4/5449d48993b0316a29237b1e741e74b65c1d29c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }3 + 2\\sqrt{3}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"112\" height=\"33\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.28quick.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_(quick)\">Solution 4 (quick)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 (quick)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/f/64fd3de28ed0935bff3c8b495fb253c6bdbe1e74.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"72\" height=\"43\" /> clearly must form a 60 degree angle with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/5/4c52e02a35db152495396584bfeae1d0599cd753.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />. The other line is the one that forms a <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/1/5c1f48bf613d84733360a370548f509eaac9060c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-60$\" width=\"31\" height=\"12\" /> degree angle with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/5/4c52e02a35db152495396584bfeae1d0599cd753.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />. Thus, it must have slope <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4f620cb0047a60cbfff5c979bdb5a528939e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"41\" height=\"41\" />, and equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/d/f/fdf5e499351092d9102eb107ffc7fdaf99674906.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y-0=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(x-0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"165\" height=\"43\" />.\n</p><p>This equation, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/5/4c52e02a35db152495396584bfeae1d0599cd753.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, and the x-axis form a 30-60-90 triangle. The length of the short leg is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/7/ca77f863e2628759e8563dc81171694f925509b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0-(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}(1))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"113\" height=\"43\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/c/e5cb6e7c42d6bf0c332b448f0d28fff6c0aaaef1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"26\" height=\"41\" />. Multiplying by 2 gives the hypotenuse and then by 3 gives the perimeter, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/4/5449d48993b0316a29237b1e741e74b65c1d29c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }3 + 2\\sqrt{3}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"112\" height=\"33\" />\n</p><p>~Stress-couture\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28coord_bash.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(coord_bash)\">Solution 5 (coord bash)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (coord bash)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Suppose that the line is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/1/cd14499bebbf7855623388869c0910c4a004bf77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=kx$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"54\" height=\"16\" />. The intersections of lines <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/1/cd14499bebbf7855623388869c0910c4a004bf77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=kx$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"54\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/5/4c52e02a35db152495396584bfeae1d0599cd753.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/1/cd14499bebbf7855623388869c0910c4a004bf77.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=kx$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"54\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/f/3bfdcd78e3b25449ce7334e043ab3f0db1c4d0b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"103\" height=\"41\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/5/4c52e02a35db152495396584bfeae1d0599cd753.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/f/3bfdcd78e3b25449ce7334e043ab3f0db1c4d0b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y=1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"103\" height=\"41\" />, are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/5/915ed196445d98be3e1c291cae5e4cbf840ff65b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,k),\\quad \\left(\\dfrac{3}{3k-\\sqrt{3}},\\dfrac{3k}{3k-\\sqrt{3}}\\right),\\quad \\left(1,\\dfrac{3+\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"374\" height=\"55\" /> respectively. Because these 3 points form an equilateral triangle, the distances between these points are equal. Therefore, we get the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/c/83c1e3d7a2778790499d860a0fdc12b5e4ae9317.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\dfrac{3}{3k-\\sqrt{3}}-1\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{3k}{3k-\\sqrt{3}}-k\\right)^2=\\left(\\dfrac{3+\\sqrt{3}}{3}-k\\right)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"446\" height=\"58\" />. Then by re-writing every term as a single fraction, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/6/7b6aedd15a4c0ee5911c7f08eab09af1ea0ccccc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(\\dfrac{3-3k+\\sqrt{3}}{3k-\\sqrt{3}}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{3k-3k^2+\\sqrt{3}\\,k}{3k-\\sqrt{3}}\\right)^2=\\left(\\dfrac{3+\\sqrt{3}-3k}{3}\\right)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"488\" height=\"58\" />.\nThen we can factor out <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/1/d31dba567d163ca5a4e39c0ffe8a6fe887892f20.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3+\\sqrt{3}-3k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"99\" height=\"21\" /> from every term, which gets us\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/c/d3cb5ce3bf745ebabbb0ba6001c422a984be40ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(3+\\sqrt{3}-3k)\\cdot\\dfrac{(3+\\sqrt{3}-3k)(1+k^2)}{(3k-\\sqrt{3})^2}=(3+\\sqrt{3}-3k)\\cdot\\dfrac{3+\\sqrt{3}-3k}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"553\" height=\"50\" />.\nCancelling out <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/1/d31dba567d163ca5a4e39c0ffe8a6fe887892f20.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3+\\sqrt{3}-3k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"99\" height=\"21\" /> gets us <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/b/bcb7edb7e8d7f8f245a81eae7b3b70633c93f9ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{1+k^2}{(3k-\\sqrt{3})^2}=\\dfrac{1}{9}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"130\" height=\"47\" />. Solving this equation gets us\n</p><p>\\begin{gather*}\n9(1+k^2) = (3k-\\sqrt{3})^2 \\\\[6pt]\n9+9k^2 = 9k^2 - 6\\sqrt{3}\\,k + 3 \\\\[6pt]\n9 = -6\\sqrt{3}\\,k + 3 \\\\[6pt]\n6 = -6\\sqrt{3}\\,k \\\\[6pt]\nk = -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\n\\end{gather*}\nNow we can find the perimeter of the equilateral triangle. Using the distance between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/8/1e82aea777197dfef8c4a3b6ff84cd57bfe9faad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\left(1,\\dfrac{3+\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"107\" height=\"55\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/b/d1ba993e46ef023f2358d2df33b7cbc526fb6bc7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(1,k)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"43\" height=\"20\" />, the side length of the equilateral triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/c/49c58a2b4d3db5123f3cbc34922cb8915ff14171.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}-\\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right) = 1+\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"247\" height=\"55\" />. Therefore, the perimeter of the triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/7/dc7cbf2d24dd77050bdfdddda767f225f0348c7f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3\\left(1+\\dfrac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\right) = 3 + 2\\sqrt{3}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -23px\" width=\"200\" height=\"55\" />, which gives us the answer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/4/5449d48993b0316a29237b1e741e74b65c1d29c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) }3 + 2\\sqrt{3}}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"112\" height=\"33\" />\n</p><p>~biegetomcat72\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/-l1Kawq_hds\">https://youtu.be/-l1Kawq_hds</a>\n</p><p>~savannahsolver\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_17&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Video Solution:\n</p><p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2kvSRL8KMac\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2kvSRL8KMac</a>\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A\">2015 AMC 10A</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Answer_Key\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_16\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 16\">Problem 16</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2015_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_18\" title=\"2015 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 18\">Problem 18</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=20